module gunbc.v1_maintenance_standing

import std.types { String }

// Why this carrier exists rather than a sentence in DESIGN.md: a status with a
// closed allow/refuse vocabulary is exactly the machine-consumed fact DESIGN 4c
// says may not live as prose. The freeze it qualifies is stated in DESIGN 3's
// frozen-X carve-out, which this module is cited from rather than duplicated into.

type SeedLifecycleStanding =
    FullyFrozen
  | SemanticsFrozen { maintenance: MaintenanceStanding }

type MaintenanceStanding =
    MaintenanceActive
  | MaintenanceClosed

// The four admitted classes. Each preserves observable semantics or carries its
// own removal event; none of them completes, extends, or widens v1.
type MaintenanceAdmission =
    BehaviorPreservingCorrectness
  | BehaviorPreservingRedundancyRemoval
  | BoundedOperationalFix
  | ChangeCarryingDeletionTrigger

// The five refused classes. These are the growth surfaces DESIGN 3 names: a
// freeze that still accepts rows is not a freeze, and MaintenanceActive does not
// reopen any of them.
type MaintenanceRefusal =
    NewLanguageBehavior
  | NewCompatibilityObligation
  | NewEscapeHatchOrAdmissionRow
  | SeedFeatureCompletion
  | PublicSurfaceGrowth

data v1_seed_standing: SeedLifecycleStanding = SemanticsFrozen { maintenance: MaintenanceActive }

data v1_maintenance_standing_ruling_note: String = "OPERATOR RULING 2026-08-18, and the reason it is a reclassification rather than a loophole. The v1 seed was carried as 'frozen' under DESIGN section 3's frozen-X carve-out: no new investment, no completion work, quarry-in-place. Work then arrived that is economically correct and that the unqualified word forbids — a measured 177.8s of repeated formal canonicalization on every required-floor run, removable by construction. The honest options were to refuse the work or to state the status accurately; describing v1 as frozen while maintaining it is the rung-inflation failure of section 4b applied to a lifecycle label, and it is worse than either option because it makes the maintenance invisible. THE ECONOMIC BASIS, which is what distinguishes this from ordinary reluctance to delete: 'let it die with v1' is only an argument when some bounded event kills the cost. gunbc#8352 closed unmerged and integration/v1-cut is quarry, so no such event is scheduled and the cost is unbounded in time. Against an eight-hour implementation the payback is 28,800 / 177.8, roughly 162 required-floor runs. THE STANDING DANGER, named so it can be checked: MaintenanceActive is a candidate absorbing arm in exactly the section 5 sense — if every proposed v1 change classifies as admissible, the vocabulary has stopped discriminating and the freeze has been repealed by drift rather than by ruling. The refused list is therefore load-bearing, not decorative, and a proposal that fits no admitted class is refused rather than accommodated by widening the list."

data v1_maintenance_vocabulary_shape_note: String = "WHY THERE IS NO VERDICT TYPE HERE, since its absence is deliberate rather than an omission. An earlier draft of this carrier declared a V1ChangeVerdict coproduct pairing the admitted and refused classes. Nothing produces it: no lens reads this module, no gate joins it to a diff, and the rung note below says plainly that classification is authored by a human reviewer rather than derived. A verdict type with no producer is uninhabited generality (DESIGN section 6, model just-in-time) and, worse, it would make this carrier LOOK like a decision procedure in a PR whose entire subject is being honest about what is and is not enforced. The two vocabularies are the whole content: they give a reviewer a closed list to classify against and a name to refuse with. If a mechanical classifier is ever built for the two decidable refused classes named in the rung note, the verdict type is minted THEN, by the thing that produces it."

data v1_maintenance_rung_note: String = "RUNG HONESTY, per DESIGN section 4b, stated because a typed vocabulary is easy to mistake for a wall. This carrier sits at MITIGATABLE: it is a declared vocabulary consumed by review diligence, and nothing mechanically refuses a v1 change that belongs to a refused class. No lens reads it, no gate joins it to a diff, and the classification is authored rather than derived — so an author who misclassifies is caught by a reviewer or not at all. That is strictly weaker than the frozen-X clause it qualifies, which at least had the virtue of admitting nothing. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, which is not a general change-classifier and deliberately so: the refused classes are not all decidable, but two of them are nearly so against the seed's own surface — PublicSurfaceGrowth is a diff over the emitted seed's exported declarations, and NewEscapeHatchOrAdmissionRow is a diff over the host-builtin registry and admission rosters DESIGN section 3 already enumerates as growth surfaces. Those two climb to mechanically preventable as a diff-grain check against the prior seed emission; NewLanguageBehavior and SeedFeatureCompletion are not decidable from a diff and stay review obligations. Nothing here claims the undecidable half will climb."

data v1_maintenance_admitted_population_note: String = "WHAT THIS STANDING HAS ADMITTED SO FAR, at exact identity grain, so the population is countable rather than a category. (1) gunbc#8431, BehaviorPreservingRedundancyRemoval: one CallApplicationPlan constructed per direct call, replacing two line-for-line duplicate per-formal prologues at the same seam; measured on a same-host sequential pair with both arms built from real refs, 2900 to 2280 peel calls on gunbc.host_effect_realize and reconcile 8 minutes to 6 minutes, with 618 of the 620 removed calls (99.7%) being repeats and the distinct-work cost invariant to +0.064%. (2) The formal-instantiation memo, same class, keyed at the semantic grain the operator specified rather than on arbitrary nodes. NOT ADMITTED, and recorded here because a refused proposal is evidence the vocabulary discriminates: removing the second peel in direct_call_structured_record_literal_resolved_type_mismatch was proposed as the same class and REFUSED on evidence — a differential control over the whole corpus found peel_nominal_alias_identity is not idempotent, so peel(peel(x)) is not a redundant call and its removal would not be behavior-preserving. The control read zero counterexamples through 240,000 calls before the first appeared, which is why the count was taken to completion rather than sampled: 21 non-idempotent results over 600,000+ calls. A span-blind rerun then classified them, because v1's Node derives equality including span and a span-only difference would have had the opposite consequence: 15 differ only in span, and 6 differ STRUCTURALLY. All six are the same shape, FreeMonoid gaining a child on the second peel (one child becoming two). Since std.string_type grounds String as FreeMonoid<Char>, the doubly-peeled node is an alias expansion that appends a type argument rather than replacing it. That makes the second peel not merely redundant-but-harmless at this seam but structurally wrong for String and FreeMonoid formals, inside a function whose output is type-mismatch diagnostics. It is recorded here and NOT fixed under this standing: repairing it changes observable diagnostics and therefore belongs to a change with its own discriminating witness, not to a behavior-preserving class."
